## 2026-08-20 (iter 20) — explorer/0.30.0: melee-predator avoidance (the REAL death cause)
Decision: add MELEE_THREAT_KINDS {golem_stone, delver, boar, drake, lake_drake} and
block the tiles ADJACENT to them in field pathing, so chars route AROUND strike range
instead of walking into it. NOT a radius-4 flee (they only hurt in melee).
Why: run #85 (0.29.0) OVERTURNED the poison-death theory. Safe-world routing works
(chars cluster in vale/mines at 0% undead, shun spire at 100%), yet deaths STAYED 2.23/1k
and 12/13 were in MINES — a 0%-UNDEAD world. Traced the killer: golem_stone hits a
full-HP char (24/24) for -15 the tick it steps adjacent, then it dies. Burst melee, not
poison DoT — which is why 0.29's heal did nothing (a potion can't outrun a 2-hit kill).
Empirical blame of big HP-drops (>=5/tick) on the nearest non-wildlife mob: delver 36,
golem_stone 28, boar 18, lake_drake 5, drake 3 = ~84% of the damage, NONE in the undead
THREAT set. The 0.25 'undead=danger, wildlife=safe' model was incomplete.
Effect/verify: deaths should fall sharply (removes ~84% of the damage source) while
looting/income HOLD (no radius flee, chars still gather everywhere but the mob's 8
neighbour tiles). FALSIFIED if deaths persist (a CHASER we mis-modelled as avoid-only —
then add a step-away when adjacent) or income craters (block strands chars from loot).
Kept 0.29 heal-from-surplus (harmless; real DoT does exist in spire). Decided
autonomously (10-min rule). NOTE the diagnostic method — empirical HP-drop-adjacency
blame — is reusable; re-run it to catch any new killer a future band brings.

