STUCK-DEATH DIAGNOSTIC (read-only, scratchpad/stuck_diag.py) — ATTEMPTED to classify WHY our
stuck chars die (cornered vs out-of-stamina vs had-exit-didnt-move). RESULT: METHOD INVALID,
self-caught. v1 said 42/42 stuck deaths were 'cornered' (0 walkable exits); v2 decomposed the
neighbours and exposed the confound: across 42 cornered deaths (168 neighbour-tiles) the make-up
was walkable 0 / SOLID 0 / occupied 28 / UNKNOWN 140. ZERO known walls + 83% unseen => the
single-frame known-map can't judge walkability (a frame's visible.tiles is sparse/incremental
BY DESIGN — the bot accumulates it into `known`; a single frame is not the char's real map). So
'cornered' is a RECONSTRUCTION ARTIFACT, NOT a finding. Did NOT record it as one.
ROBUST facts that survive: 80% of deaths are STUCK (position-based, tile-independent); ~37%
(19/51) had a predator adjacent at the death frame. CORRECTION to the iter37 recommendation
framing: I do NOT yet know the stuck MECHANISM (terrain-corner vs mob-pin vs decision-failure),
so a 0.37 anti-stuck DESIGN is premature. Proper next diagnostic (read-only, next pass): stream
each world's field frames in tick order accumulating the known-map, snapshot each death char's
neighbours against THAT map + predator distances -> then the cornered/decision split is valid.
DECISION: still recommend a 0.37 anti-stuck lever to the operator, but gated on that accumulated-
map diagnostic first (don't design blind). Held for greenlight; no strategy change shipped.
